WEBVTT

o1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:05.300
This is one function, battle. It plays a small card fight to the end.

o2
00:00:06.250 --> 00:00:09.450
Each turn, it draws a hand of cards,

o3
00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:12.450
shows the turn on the screen,

o4
00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:16.200
lets the player choose a card, and plays it.

o5
00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:20.350
Then dice decide the enemy's move.

o6
00:00:21.050 --> 00:00:25.900
It goes round until one side is out of HP, and returns who won.

o7.1
00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:29.867
Show, Choose, Played and Roll are effects.

o7.2
00:00:29.867 --> 00:00:36.450
At each yield, battle pauses and hands the effect out to a handler. It never says which one.

o8
00:00:37.250 --> 00:00:43.600
The handler also gets the rest of battle, called k, and resumes it with an answer.

o9
00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:51.700
Stack handlers around it. A scripted player and a screen give the same fight on every run.

o10
00:00:52.750 --> 00:00:56.600
Swap in a random bot and take the screen away. The function does not change.

o11
00:01:00.250 --> 00:01:05.900
Add a recorder, and thirty-two answers replay the whole fight, with no dice and no player.

o12
00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:18.200
A Monte Carlo bot answers a question by running the fight itself, while the fight that asked waits as k.

o13.1
00:01:22.250 --> 00:01:25.683
Run a hundred fights at once, each with its own dice.

o13.2
00:01:25.683 --> 00:01:30.600
The random bot wins seventy-three. The Monte Carlo bot wins all one hundred.

o14
00:01:38.650 --> 00:01:45.000
One function, unchanged, in every stack. That is algebraic effects in Python, with doeff.
